  "question": {
    "text": "A body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> is attached to one end of a wire of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. What is the maximum angular speed (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>) with which it can be rotated about its other end in a space station without breaking the wire?<br />[Breaking stress of wire&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>&#963;</mi></mfenced></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4.8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> and area of cross-section of the wire<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>]"
  },
